# devbox Project

## Directory Structure
Based on standard Go project structure:
- cmd/: Entry points for command line tools.
- internal/: Packages used internally within the project.
- pkg/: Packages available for external use. Contains the built executable files.
- scripts/: Directory containing shell scripts for building Go files. No Go files exist here.
- util/: Utility functions.

## `internal` package
The internal package contains private packages organized by functionality. Each CLI tool has its corresponding internal package.

The actual structure follows a per-tool organization pattern where each tool has its own package under `internal/`. The structure varies by tool complexity:

- Simple tools: Only `usecases/` directory
- Complex tools: `domain/`, `interfaces/`, `usecases/` directories following clean architecture
- Specialized tools: Custom structure based on specific needs

Architecture patterns used:
- Clean Architecture Pattern: For complex tools with `domain/`, `interfaces/`, `usecases/`
- Simple Pattern: For straightforward tools with `usecases/` only
- Custom Pattern: For specialized tools with unique requirements

The implementation follows these principles:
- `domain/`: Business entities and rules (cannot import external packages)
- `interfaces/`: Interface adapters (can import external packages)  
- `usecases/`: Application business logic (can import external packages)
- Functional programming is generally recommended over procedural programming

## `cmd` package
The overview of each entry point is as follows:
- api-client: Make requests to APIs with any method, using any JSON file as the request body. Can also set a token in the Authorization header.
- env-loader: Set environment variables described in any yml file.
- file-processor: 
- json-modifier: Set any value (string or integer or floating-point number) to any new key in a single JSON file.
- json-timestamp-modifier: Set the current date and time's UNIX timestamp to any new key in a single JSON file.
